{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c59f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a074a4-ddb9-7301-870e-62d9c91b70f4"},"sequence":298,"recorded_at":1780531400000591,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"445757ea-edf1-42a4-8d03-75b0e90ee6ce","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"445757ea-edf1-42a4-8d03-75b0e90ee6ce","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"445757ea-edf1-42a4-8d03-75b0e90ee6ce"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"f550c1d7-2ab3-4f85-9918-241635e4451a"},"task_id":"f550c1d7-2ab3-4f85-9918-241635e4451a","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"f550c1d7-2ab3-4f85-9918-241635e4451a","chunk":"Read text file `/tmp/rev4-verdict.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-2z3se0 — reviewer verdict, CR-TASK-260830-2z3se0-4 revision 4\n2|\n3|- Verdict: **changes requested** → `to-dev`\n4|- repeat-of: `rev1/F7, rev2/N4, rev3/B4` (B7, fourth round on the zero-value /\n5|  absent-fact class) and `rev2 (unmeasured), rev3/B5` (B8, second round on the\n6|  bound class). Both are past the routing threshold, so both requested changes\n7|  below are **censuses**, not site lists. B6's requested gate was delivered and\n8|  works; it is not repeated.\n9|- Reviewed tree: candidate `1469c6348e064454dd31a5a727e25486ed467b1b` (base\n10|  `1cb6b93585749df4ef4755ce93e486bcde9e3a6b`), `repository_delta=present`, 32 paths.\n11|- Working-tree OID recomputed before the first mutant, after every batch, and after\n12|  the scratch probe file was deleted; unchanged at the end\n13|  (`1469c6348e…`), `git status --short` identical to the handoff (6 modified, 1 untracked).\n14|\n15|## Baseline reproduced by this run\n16|\n17|| Check | Result |\n18|| --- | --- |\n19|| `go build ./...` | exit 0 |\n20|| `go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n21|| `gofmt -l internal/` | empty |\n22|| `go test ./... -count=1` | 17/17 packages ok |\n23|| `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck` | ok, `acceptance_cases=88`, exit 0 |\n24|| 7 session-adapter acceptance cases → named tests | **7 of 7 resolve to real `func Test…` declarations** |\n25|\n26|Every gate the rework note claims is reproduced. The producer's numbers are honest.\n27|\n28|## Correction to the brief's premise\n29|\n30|The brief warned that \"this round touched production in several gates, so\n31|[resurrection] is a live risk.\" It did not. The rev3→rev4 delta is:\n32|\n33|```\n34|LOGBOOK.md                                 |  11 +\n35|internal/sessadapter/bounds_edge_test.go   | 423 +\n36|internal/sessadapter/import_census_test.go | 126 +\n37|internal/sessadapter/inventory_test.go     |  53 +-\n38|internal/sessadapter/zero_absent_test.go   | 196 +\n39|```\n40|\n41|**Zero production Go source changed.** The three new CR paths (29 → 32) are\n42|exactly those three new test files. Resurrection was still swept for — 76\n43|previously-killed mutants re-measured, see G-C — but the structural risk the\n44|brief anticipated was not present.\n45|\n46|## G-A — B6 is closed, and closed as a census\n47|\n48|The fix is an import census derived from production, not a spelling list and not\n49|a fourth site patch. `TestAxerrorImportsAreUnaliased` reads the package directory,\n50|parses every non-test `.go` file, and requires **every** import of the error path\n51|to bind the identifier `axerror`; anything else is a violation. It fails closed on\n52|zero scanned files and on zero importers. There is no alias enumeration anywhere,\n53|so there is no \"fifth spelling\".\n54|\n55|Probed against production, not read:\n56|\n57|| Probe | Result |\n58|| --- | --- |\n59|| A3 — second aliased import `axe …/axerror` in protocol.go + `axe.New` additive arm | **KILLED** — `TestAxerrorImportsAreUnaliased` |\n60|| A4 — fresh aliased import in manifest.go (imports no axerror today) + `axe.New` arm | **KILLED** — same |\n61|| A5 — dot import `. …/axerror` + bare `New(...)` arm | **KILLED** — same |\n62|| A9 — alias present with **no** arm at all | **KILLED** — the census fires on the import alone |\n63|| A7 control — unregistered 8th package-level constructor + additive arm | **KILLED** — `TestRefusalConstructorsMatchProduction` |\n64|| A8 control — inline `axerror.New` refusal, no constructor | **KILLED** — same |\n65|\n66|**6 of 7. Both rev3 controls still dead — no resurrection in the constructor census.**\n67|B6 is resolved and must not be re-litigated.\n68|\n69|The seventh is recorded below as N8: it is a different mechanism, not a fifth spelling.\n70|\n71|## G-B — B4 and B5 closed as **sites**, not as classes\n72|\n73|Both named survivor lists are 100% closed. Both classes, measured over a\n74|denominator derived from production, are roughly half open.\n75|\n76|### B4 — the 13 named sites: 13 of 13. The class: 22 of 48.\n77|\n78|The 13 sites the rev3 verdict named all die now:\n79|\n80|| Named site | Killed by |\n81|| --- | --- |\n82|| 10 envelope echo gates (protocol.go 278/285/384/391/398/405/474/481/488/495) | `TestEnvelopeEchoEmptyStringRefuses/{request,success,failure}/…` — 10 of 10 |\n83|| `capabilityMapUsable` absent ⇒ usable | `TestCapabilityUsableAbsentIsNotUsable` + `TestCheckTargetWriteGatesAbsentAdapterRefuses` |\n84|| `CapabilityUsable` absent ⇒ usable | `TestCapabilityUsableAbsentIsNotUsable` |\n85|| `CheckDoctorHealthy` exempts `\"\"` | `TestCheckDoctorHealthyRefusesEmptyRequiredName` |\n86|| provider-half absence (control) | `TestCheckTargetWriteGates` |\n87|\n88|The class denominator is every `X != Y` refusal comparison in production, taken\n89|by traversal. **48 applied, 22 killed, 25 survived (1 further mutant excluded as\n90|equivalent, 1 unmeasured).**\n91|\n92|| Survivor group | Sites | Survived |\n93|| --- | ---: | ---: |\n94|| `DecodeProbe` schema / schema_version echo (probe.go:257, :264) | 2 | 2 |\n95|| `CheckProbe` identity facts (probe.go:392, :399, :406, :413, :420, :427) | 6 | 6 |\n96|| `CheckProbeRequest` expected provider (probe.go:443) | 1 | 1 |\n97|| doctor direction echo, doctor entry status (probe.go:610, :685) | 2 | 2 |\n98|| `sealedMatchesFresh` seal arms (discovery.go:172–177) | 6 | 6 |\n99|| `Discover` manifest provider (discovery.go:98) | 1 | 1 |\n100|| `CheckTupleAdmission` key/status arms (tuple.go:1248, :1255, :1262, :1264, :1273) | 5 | 5 |\n101|| call-context request digest (context.go:198) | 1 | 1 |\n102|| `checkValidateResult` mode echo (operations.go:1091) | 1 | **excluded — equivalent** (the vocabulary gate at :1084 refuses `\"\"` first) |\n103|| capture-plan digest echo (operations.go:810) | 1 | unmeasured (`scalar.Digest` has no in-package zero literal) |\n104|\n105|`DecodeProbe`'s schema echo is the headline, because it is the *identical* two\n106|lines as `DecodeManifest`'s schema echo — which **is** pinned — and it is reachable\n107|from raw bytes. Driven at the production entry, baseline vs the one-token narrowing:\n108|\n109|```\n110|baseline : DecodeProbe(schema=\"\") -> session_adapter_protocol_error:\n111|           adapter probe schema is not the session adapter probe\n112|mutant   : DecodeProbe(schema=\"\") -> err=<nil>\n113|full package suite under the mutant: ok\n114|```\n115|\n116|A probe body that does not name the session adapter probe schema at all is fully\n117|admitted, and nothing reddens.\n118|\n119|Why the fix did not reach it: `TestEnvelopeEchoEmptyStringRefuses` derives its rows\n120|from `requestRequired[:2]`, `successRequired[:4]`, `failureRequired[:4]` and pins\n121|`rows != 10`. That is a derivation **inside a hand-picked domain of three\n122|envelopes**. It cannot grow to a fourth decoder, and its own tripwire forbids it.\n123|The `CheckProbe`/`sealedMatchesFresh`/`CheckTupleAdmission` rows are exported gates\n124|taking caller-supplied structs — the same standard the already-fixed\n125|`CheckCallBinding` rows were held to in rev2/N4 — but I did not drive each through\n126|a decode path, so their reachability from bytes is **unknown**, not asserted.\n127|\n128|### B5 — every `checkStringBounds` edge: 35 of 35. The bound class: 53 of 101.\n129|\n130|The rev3 request was \"pin every `checkStringBounds` maximum and every array cap at\n131|the edge\", and that is exactly and completely done:\n132|\n133|| Family | Applied | Killed | Survived |\n134|| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n135|| `checkStringBounds` maxima (all 18 production sites) | 18 | **18** | 0 |\n136|| `checkStringBounds` minima (17) | 17 | **17** | 0 |\n137|| explicit `len` caps + lower edges (dispositions, argv, events, strategies, fidelity limits, contracts, versions) | 11 | **11** | 0 |\n138|| `MaxFrameBytes` ×3 | 3 | **3** | 0 |\n139|| `checkUint53Bounds` finite maxima (context.go:746, :754) | 2 | **2** | 0 |\n140|| **`requireStringBounds` maxima (operations.go, 13 sites)** | 13 | 0 | **13** |\n141|| **`requireStringBounds` minima (13 sites)** | 13 | 1 | **12** |\n142|| **`requireUint53Bounds` finite maxima (4 sites)** | 4 | 0 | **4** |\n143|| **`checkSortedUniqueStrings` element + count maxima (14)** | 14 | 1 | **13** (2 of them equivalent) |\n144|| **`checkSortedUniqueDigests` count maxima (4)** | 4 | 0 | **4** |\n145|| **reverse-DNS extension-key bounds (decode.go:441, both edges)** | 2 | 0 | **2** |\n146|| Total | **101** | **53** | **48** (46 real) |\n147|\n148|`requireStringBounds` is 25 of the 46. It is the wrapper around `checkStringBounds`\n149|declared 100 lines above the sites that were pinned, called from `CheckRequestBody`\n150|and `CheckSuccessBody` — the operation-layer production entries. Driven, baseline vs\n151|`+1`:\n152|\n153|```\n154|baseline : CheckSuccessBody(OpResumePlan, cwd_relative=4096 chars) -> nil\n155|           CheckSuccessBody(OpResumePlan, cwd_relative=4097 chars) -> session_adapter_protocol_error:\n156|                operation body string member is outside its bound\n157|mutant   : requireStringBounds(members,\"cwd_relative\",1,4097) -> 4097 admitted, err=<nil>\n158|\n159|baseline : CheckRequestBody(OpResumePlan, expected_target_native_session_id=513) -> refused\n160|mutant   : requireStringBounds(...,1,513) -> 513 admitted, err=<nil>\n161|full package suite under both mutants: ok\n162|```\n163|\n164|Two mutants excluded as **equivalent**, not counted as gaps: `platforms` element\n165|max 16→17 and count 4→5 are both unreachable behind `scalar.ParsePlatform` and the\n166|four-name closed set. Five survivors (`created_resource_keys` count and the four\n167|`checkSortedUniqueDigests` counts, all 65536) need a 65537-element body and are\n168|fair **stated-bound** candidates rather than required rows — say so explicitly\n169|rather than leaving them in the denominator silently. The other 41 are cheap: a\n170|513-character string, or the literal number 65537.\n171|\n172|## G-C — the traversal, the ratio, and the resurrections\n173|\n174|Denominators derived from production by traversal, not from the prior verdict.\n175|\n176|| Class | Applied | Killed | Survived |\n177|| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n178|| Vocabulary / member tables widened by one (all 63 census registrations) | 63 | **63** | 0 |\n179|| Obligation loops + capability conjunction + validate applicable triple | 11 | **11** | 0 |\n180|| Import / constructor census probes | 7 | 6 | 1 |\n181|| Zero-value / absent-fact identity gates | 48 | 22 | 26 |\n182|| Numeric / string bound guards, both edges | 101 | 53 | 48 |\n183|| Malformed probe of my own (see below) | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n184|| **Total** | **231** | **155** | **76** |\n185|\n186|**155 narrowing mutants killed of 231 applied (67%).** Of the 76 survivors, one is\n187|a malformed probe of mine — `GC-B1 restore defect` planted an arbitrary extra rule\n188|rather than the actual rev3 B1 shape, so its survival measures nothing and is\n189|discarded — and 3 are equivalent mutants named above. **72 real survivors.**\n190|\n191|Round 3 was 96 of 131. The ratio moved because the traversal is wider, not because\n192|the work regressed: rev3's 32-mutant bound denominator did not reach\n193|`requireStringBounds`, `requireUint53Bounds`, `checkSortedUniqueStrings`, or\n194|`checkSortedUniqueDigests`, and its 10-mutant echo denominator did not reach any\n195|decoder outside the three protocol envelopes. Where the two traversals overlap,\n196|this round is strictly better.\n197|\n198|### Round-3 survivors: 35 of 35 addressed. Resurrections: none.\n199|\n200|- All 35 rev3 survivors were re-probed. The 13 B4 sites and the 20 B5 bounds are\n201|  killed; the 2 B6 alias probes are killed by the new import census.\n202|- **Resurrection sweep: 76 previously-killed mutants re-measured, 0 resurrected.**\n203|  All 63 vocabulary tables still die when widened by one member (`63 of 63`); the\n204|  `DoctorRequiredCapabilities` and `CheckTargetWriteGates` obligation loops die on\n205|  every drop (`9 of 9`); `checkValidateResult`'s applicable triple dies on both\n206|  shrinks; both constructor-census controls still die.\n207|- This is expected and confirmed: the round changed no production source.\n208|\n209|## Blocking findings\n210|\n211|### B7 (blocking) — the zero-value / absent-fact class is closed at the 13 named sites and open at 25 more, including a decoder whose twin is pinned\n212|\n213|`repeat-of: rev1/F7 (4 sites, closed), rev2/N4 (5 sites, closed), rev3/B4 (13\n214|sites, closed this round)`. **Fourth consecutive round, same class, new sites, same\n215|shape of fix.** Evidence and the driven `DecodeProbe` proof are in G-B above.\n216|\n217|The routing rule's threshold was passed two rounds ago, and B6 shows what clearing\n218|it looks like: the producer built a census and the class closed permanently in one\n219|round. B7 has not had that. `TestEnvelopeEchoEmptyStringRefuses` is a fixed\n220|ten-row domain with a tripwire that forbids growth, so a fifth round of naming\n221|sites produces a fifth fixed table.\n222|\n223|### B8 (blocking) — the bound class is closed at `checkStringBounds` and open at 46 guards reached through five other helpers\n224|\n225|`repeat-of: rev2 (claimed traversal, class not measured), rev3/B5 (20 sites, closed\n226|this round)`. Evidence and two driven production-entry proofs in G-B above.\n227|\n228|`TestStringBoundEdges` is a hand-written 17-row literal with `if len(rows) != 17`.\n229|It pins precisely the rows the previous reviewer listed and, like the echo table,\n230|its own tripwire prevents it from growing. `TestArrayBoundEdges` is four\n231|hand-written subtests over the four caps rev3 named. Neither table can notice that\n232|production has 31 more bound call sites through `requireStringBounds`,\n233|`requireUint53Bounds`, `checkSortedUniqueStrings`, and `checkSortedUniqueDigests` —\n234|all six helpers are enumerable with one grep.\n235|\n236|## Non-blocking findings\n237|\n238|### N8 — the constructor census still admits a package-level function-value indirection\n239|\n240|The import census closed every alias spelling. It does not close a call that never\n241|names `axerror` at the call site:\n242|\n243|```go\n244|var newFault = axerror.New                       // import census: green (unaliased)\n245|...\n246|shadow, err := newFault(axerror.Spec{...})       // constructor census: invisible\n247|```\n248|\n249|`constructorSitesInFile` matches a `*ast.CallExpr` whose `Fun` is a\n250|`SelectorExpr` on the identifier `axerror`; `pairedFuncLit` returns nil for a\n251|`SelectorExpr` value, so the definition creates no scope and the call creates no\n252|site. Planted as an additive arm in `DecodeRequestFrame`, the full package suite is\n253|green (probe GA-A6). The arm derivation misses it too, so a refusal added this way\n254|ships unwitnessed.\n255|\n256|This is **not** a fifth alias spelling and not a repeat of B6 — B6's requested gate\n257|was built and works. It is the same prose-ahead-of-the-check habit as N6: the\n258|census sentence says \"every `axerror.New` **site** in production must sit inside one\n259|registered constructor body\", and the check reads call sites only. Either widen the\n260|match to any `axerror.New` selector outside a registered body, or narrow the\n261|sentence and declare the indirection as a stated bound. It needs no round of its\n262|own; fold it into whichever round closes B7/B8.\n263|\n264|### N9 — the battery is finding-scoped for the third consecutive round\n265|\n266|`repeat-of: rev2 requested-change #6, rev3/N7.` The round-4 note reports **36 of 36\n267|killed** with an accurate, honest method bound (\"battery traverses the reviewer's 35\n268|survivors + the N6 arm\"). Every one of those 36 reproduces, and stating the bound\n269|instead of implying a traversal is the right instinct — I am counting it in the\n270|producer's favour, as rev3 did.\n271|\n272|But a declared bound closes a *blind spot*; it does not close a *class*. The\n273|difference between N5's declared blind spots (regexp alternation, `init()`-populated\n274|maps — genuinely hard to see) and this one is that here the unmeasured surface is\n275|36 call sites of six named helpers, enumerable with a single grep, in the same\n276|class as the finding being fixed. 36 of 36 and 155 of 230 describe the same tree.\n277|\n278|## What is correct and must not be re-litigated\n279|\n280|- **B6 is closed as a census.** Production-derived, no spelling list, fail-closed on\n281|  zero scanned files and zero importers, and it kills the alias, the fresh alias,\n282|  the dot import, and a bare alias with no arm. Exactly the gate that was asked for.\n283|- **All 13 B4 sites and all 35 `checkStringBounds` edges are genuinely pinned**, at\n284|  the production entry, with the killer named per row. Nothing in B7/B8 disputes\n285|  any of it.\n286|- **All 63 vocabulary tables remain content-pinned**, 63 of 63, and the obligation\n287|  loops, capability conjunction, and validate triple are 11 of 11. This is still\n288|  the strongest evidence in the package.\n289|- **Zero resurrections** across 76 re-measured mutants, and the round changed no\n290|  production source.\n291|- **N6 is closed** — the function-local registered-name case reports, pinned by a\n292|  synthetic vector that says it is synthetic.\n293|- **Traceability and README are honest.** `acceptance_cases=88`, all 7\n294|  session-adapter cases resolve to real tests, and the README states plainly that\n295|  the §7.8 clause-level binding stays `unevidenced` and the registry makes no\n296|  clause-level claim. No unsupported capability is advertised.\n297|\n298|## Requested changes\n299|\n300|Two censuses, modelled on `TestClosedVocabularyTablesAreRegistered` — the one gate\n301|in this package that has held at 63 of 63 across three rounds. Both are the same\n302|shape: derive the denominator from production source, require a row per subject,\n303|fail closed on an unrostered subject.\n304|\n305|1. **B8 — a bound census.** Parse the production files, collect every call to\n306|   `checkStringBounds`, `requireStringBounds`, `checkUint53Bounds`,\n307|   `requireUint53Bounds`, `checkSortedUniqueStrings`, `checkSortedUniqueDigests`\n308|   and every explicit `len(...) > N` / `len(...) == 0` guard. Require each site to\n309|   carry an edge row driven at min-1/min/max/max+1 through its production entry, or\n310|   an explicit exemption row saying why. A new bound with no row fails the census.\n311|   The 46 real survivors are listed by site in G-B; the five 65536-count rows are a\n312|   legitimate stated bound if you say so.\n313|2. **B7 — an identity-gate census.** Same shape over every `X != Y` refusal\n314|   comparison and every map-presence default in production. Require a zero/absent\n315|   row driven at the exported entry, or a declared exemption naming the upstream\n316|   guard that makes the zero unreachable — `checkValidateResult`'s mode echo is a\n317|   real example of a legitimate exemption, and `platforms` is the equivalent-mutant\n318|   analogue on the bound side. Replace the `rows != 10` tripwire with the census\n319|   count so the table cannot go stale.\n320|3. **N8** — widen the constructor census to any `axerror.New` selector outside a\n321|   registered body, or narrow its sentence and declare the indirection.\n322|4. **Evidence** — report the next battery as a ratio over the two censuses'\n323|   denominators. Once they exist this is mechanical: the census *is* the\n324|   denominator, which is the whole point of asking for one instead of a table.\n325|\n326|After these two censuses exist, a new bound or a new identity gate cannot ship\n327|unrostered, and this class stops consuming rounds. That is why the request is two\n328|gates and not 71 rows.\n329|\n330|## Stop-the-line\n331|\n332|None. Both findings are ordinary, bounded, mechanical rework inside this package.\n333|The censuses have a working model in the same test suite, the denominators are one\n334|grep each, and no external constraint or human product/architecture/approval\n335|decision is involved.\n336|\n337|## Method and integrity\n338|\n339|- 231 narrowing mutants applied by exact-string or exact-line replacement, each\n340|  grep-confirmed present on disk, each `go vet`-clean before its verdict, each\n341|  reverted immediately after; 8 further mutants recorded `NOT_APPLIED` with the\n342|  reason (type-zero literals unavailable in the file, or a shifted anchor) and\n343|  excluded from every ratio rather than counted as passes. One applied mutant\n344|  (`GC-B1 restore defect`) was a malformed probe on my side and its survival is\n345|  reported as measuring nothing rather than as a finding.\n346|- 3 survivors re-examined and reclassified as **equivalent mutants** after finding\n347|  the upstream guard that makes them unreachable; they are excluded from the\n348|  finding, not from the denominator.\n349|- One scratch test file (`zz_reviewer_probe_test.go`) drove baseline-vs-mutant\n350|  behaviour at `DecodeProbe`, `CheckRequestBody`, `CheckSuccessBody`, and\n351|  `DecodeManifest`; deleted in the same call.\n352|- Working-tree OID recomputed after every batch and at the end:\n353|  `1469c6348e064454dd31a5a727e25486ed467b1b`, equal to the candidate;\n354|  `git status --short` unchanged.\n355|- `go test ./... -count=1` re-run on the restored tree: 17/17 packages ok."}}}
